#{[17592186249051 :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}x+3\\operatorname{sec}x+C"] [17592186249161 :answer/value "-4\\operatorname{cos}x-3\\operatorname{cot}x+C"] [17592186249217 :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cot}\\theta +C"] [17592186249233 :answer/value "{tan}^{2}\\theta +C"] [17592186249349 :answer/value "2\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C"] [17592186249513 :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}\\theta +C"] [17592186249539 :answer/value "3\\operatorname{cos}x-2\\operatorname{sin}x+C"] [17592186249596 :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}x-2\\operatorname{cos}x+C"] [17592186250195 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{arcsec}\\,x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-5\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186250196 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\text{arcsec}\\,x.\n$$\n\nThen we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{12}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-12\\int \\frac{1}{|x|\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-1}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-12\\operatorname{arcsec} x+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186250237 :question/worked-solution "To solve this integral, we use the by-parts formula, given by\n\n$$\n\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x=uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe need to express $\\displaystyle \\int x{csc}^{2}⁡x\\text{d}x$ as $\\displaystyle \\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Therefore we set\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,{u}^{′}=1 \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{csc}^{2}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute the above into the integration by parts formula, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int u{v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =uv-\\int v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int x\\cdot \\operatorname{csc}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =x\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the integral using the substitution $u=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ where $\\text{d}u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{csc}^{2}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\int \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =-x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186253732 :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡x"] [17592186254291 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡x"] [17592186255544 :answer/value "v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡x"] [17592186255726 :answer/value "4v\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡x"] [17592186255747 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡x"] [17592186256601 :question/problem "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis where $x>0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin $O$ is $x$ meters, its velocity is $v\\,m/s$ and its acceleration is $\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡x\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ Given that the acceleration is directed toward $O,$ which differential equation describes this motion?"] [17592186256601 :question/worked-solution "![](images/76/768db6b8051d8f4bea528f6c5b5d386ffc0d6529517d2fe72641cb33f6e8ac10.png)\n\nSince the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡x$ and is directed *toward* $O,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule for differentiation, we can rewrite this equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we obtain the differential equation\n\n$$\nv\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$"] [17592186265711 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$"] [17592186265711 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(0,-1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(0,-1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-1}{0+1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265712 :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-2)}\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1}.$"] [17592186265712 :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(1,-2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-2)}\\sqrt[3]{3x-2y+1} & =\\sqrt[3]{3(1)-2(-2)+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{3+4+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186265713 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.$"] [17592186265713 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{(-1)(2)+1(2)}{(-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2+2}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{xz+yz}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z(x+y)}{(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x+y)}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x+y)}(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1,2)}\\frac{z}{x-y} & =\\frac{2}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-1.$"] [17592186265714 :question/problem "Determine $$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}}.\n$$"] [17592186265714 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{2}^{3}-3({2}^{2})(2)+3(2)({2}^{2})-{2}^{3}}{{2}^{2}-2(2)(2)+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-24+24-8}{4-8+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+3x{y}^{2}-{y}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-2xy+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{(x-y)^{3}}{(x-y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{(x-y)(x-y)^{2}}{(x-y)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}\\frac{(x-y)\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x-y)^{2}}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x-y)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}(x-y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,2)}(x-y) & =2-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $0.$"] [17592186265715 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy}.$"] [17592186265715 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\frac{(-1)^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}-(-1)(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-1}{1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x-y)}{x(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x-y)}}{x\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x-y)}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{x+y}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,-1)}\\frac{x+y}{x} & =\\frac{-1+(-1)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)}{(-1)} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$"] [17592186265716 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}}=\n$$"] [17592186265716 :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{0}-\\sqrt{0}+0-0}{\\sqrt{0}-\\sqrt{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0+0-0}{0-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y}{\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y}+x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-\\sqrt{y})(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})+(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{x-y+(x-y)(\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{(x-y)(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{x-y} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{(x-y)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{x-y}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}(1+\\sqrt{x}+\\sqrt{y}) & =1+\\sqrt{0}+\\sqrt{0} \\\\  & =1+0+0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $1.$"]}
